course: dictionary
lesson: 12902
title: Building words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: In history and building topics you may meet the word erection. It is a formal word for putting something up. «The erection of the wall took three months.» The clue is the wall: a wall must be built, so erection means the act of building or putting up.
explain: Everyday speech usually says putting up or building. «Workers began the erection of the stage before lunch» means they began putting up the stage. Swap in your everyday word and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Look at the clue words around it: statue, tent, wall, monument, scaffolding. These tell you the topic is about building or putting something up, so you can work out the meaning even when the word is new.
fact: Erection means the act of building or putting something up.
fact: «The erection of a monument» means the building of a monument.
fact: «Erection of scaffolding» means putting up the scaffolding around a building.
fact: Erection can also mean the building or structure itself, but this use is formal.
exercise: «The erection of the wall took three months.» What does erection mean here? | A sleepy yawn | Taking something down | *The act of building or putting up | A kind of paint
exercise: Which everyday phrase could replace erection in «the erection of the stage»? | Losing the stage | Sweeping the stage | *Putting up the stage | Painting the stage
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Workers started the erection of the tents before lunch» that names the things being put up. | *tents | before | lunch | workers
test: «The erection of the scaffolding took two days.» What does erection mean? | Painting | Sleeping | *Putting up | Taking away
test: What everyday word could replace erection in «the erection of a monument»? | Losing | Hiding | Breaking | *Building
exam: «The erection of the new bridge began in May.» What does erection mean? | Ending | Cooking | *Building | Shouting
